id: decisions/002-coverage-allocation
title: "Where proof and memory get spent"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'coverage-allocation'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: coverage-allocation
axis_gloss: "whether verification, indexing and specification are spread evenly over everything, or concentrated on a chosen few"
poles:
  a: selective-concentrated-coverage
  b: exhaustive-even-coverage
split: leaning
draft_status: full
leverage: 53.8
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(64) x split_weight(0.7) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  Should every record, loop and receipt get the same depth of proof, memory and
  indexing, or should the heavy machinery be concentrated on the few that can
  spend, publish or mutate, and should that choice be written down?

why_it_matters: >
  Today the repo spends its heaviest checks on one unscheduled image loop and on
  receipt schemas, while 66 of 194 files carry no proof block and only four
  automations keep memory; nothing says this is deliberate. Once answered, a new
  bot knows whether it must ship with a memory root, an approval contract and a
  test before it runs, or only when it can act outside the repo.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    61 judgments sit at selective-concentrated-coverage (1 authored) and 3 at
    exhaustive-even-coverage (0 authored). The repository asserts both directions
    on this axis.
  attributed_to: TAXONOMY.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether the twice-daily AI-writing scan (J0034) reflects something Dave cares
    about more than license or comprehension risk, or is an accident of when it
    was written; the criterion for concentration is unknown until he names it.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      Coverage is concentrated. A record, loop or schema gets a runnable proof, a
      memory root and a full approval contract only when it can spend money,
      publish, send, or mutate canonical state; every other record declares
      proof.status: seed-backed or none, and the criterion is written once in
      agents/yawn.bot.policy.yawn.
    cost: "One paragraph in the policy file; a status line on the 66 files that have no proof block."
    risk: "A loop that quietly gains an external effect keeps its light coverage until someone notices."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend tests/interception-statement-image-governance.test.mjs to scan every automation/*.yawn: any contract whose allowed_effects include spend, publish, send or mutate must carry an approval contract and a memory root; the rest need not."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0034"
      - "J0037"
      - "J0038"
      - "J0057"
      - "J0106"
      - "J0128"
  - name: B
    description: >
      Coverage is even. Every automation carries canonical_memory and a ledger,
      every record carries a proof block, and every receipt records every gate
      against every axis whether or not it fired.
    cost: "Memory roots for nine more automations, proof blocks for 66 files, promotion of every v0-seed core file to a proof condition."
    risk: "Proof blocks written to satisfy a rule rather than a claim; seed-backed becomes wallpaper."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "A new tests/proof-coverage.test.mjs asserting every .yawn has a top-level proof block and every automation/*.yawn has canonical_memory; today it would fail on 66 and 9 files respectively."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0323"
      - "J1089"
      - "J1243"
  - name: C
    description: >
      Coverage is concentrated across records but exhaustive inside a receipt. Which
      records get heavy machinery follows option A's criterion; but any receipt or
      migration record that exists to prove something enumerates everything it
      assessed, including gates that did not fire and paths that were not changed.
    cost: "Same as A plus one sentence in schemas/node.yawn stating the receipt rule."
    risk: "Receipts stay large (45 gate assessments per selection, 340 lines per migration) and readers skip them."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Option A's scan, plus a test that every *receipt*.schema.json keeps minItems equal to maxItems on its gate and axis arrays (tests/inquiry-selection-receipt-v0.1.test.mjs already pins 5 and 9)."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0034"
      - "J0037"
      - "J0057"
      - "J0323"
      - "J1089"
      - "J1243"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.7
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    Both proof-attached judgments (J1089, J1243) are exhaustive and both live
    inside receipt schemas and their tests; every selective judgment is a reading
    of unlabelled behaviour across records, and the artifacts, not Dave,
    concentrate coverage. GROUND_TRUTH FACT 17a shows the one test-enforced
    allocation: the only loop that can spend or publish is the only one held
    unscheduled behind a one-time approval. The tree already does C; nothing
    states it. Option A alone would license trimming receipts the tests currently
    forbid; option B is contradicted by 66 proof-less files and would only be
    honoured by wallpaper.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "agents/yawn.bot.policy.yawn states which capabilities (spend, publish, send, mutate canonical state) require an approval contract, a memory root and a test before scheduling"
    - "A test in tests/ rejects any automation contract that has those capabilities without those three things, and passes"
    - "Every receipt schema under schemas/ keeps minItems equal to maxItems on gate and axis arrays, asserted by test"
    - "The 66 files without a proof block either gain one or carry proof.status: none-by-design"

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A new automation acquires an email, publish or spend effect and ships with
    schedule set and no approval contract, while a prose-only audit loop gets a
    memory root and a ledger first.
  status: proposed

evidence_sample:
  - "J0034"
  - "J0037"
  - "J0038"
  - "J0057"
  - "J0106"
  - "J0128"
  - "J0323"
  - "J1089"
  - "J1243"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
  id_index: TAXONOMY.md
